{"authored" [{"question" "q-138901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)={e}^{2t}-{t}^{3}$ meters.\nFind the expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc8f891e15b0533a7f308f4e84fd0c915502ec32777d09b81e5382286977ae17" "basis" 1485 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-138901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}-{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}-3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{2t}-3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{2t}-6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force in Newtons is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =3\\cdot (4{e}^{2t}-6t) \\\\  & =12{e}^{2t}-18t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc8f891e15b0533a7f308f4e84fd0c915502ec32777d09b81e5382286977ae17" "basis" 1485 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-138901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7ad419d93ee9149ffc25caab33e9435e5bfce01f2e6915a31407868c03eb4de" "basis" 1485 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-138901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc8f891e15b0533a7f308f4e84fd0c915502ec32777d09b81e5382286977ae17" "basis" 1485 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-138901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "12{e}^{2t}-18t"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc8f891e15b0533a7f308f4e84fd0c915502ec32777d09b81e5382286977ae17" "basis" 1485 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-138901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "12{e}^{2t}-6t"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc8f891e15b0533a7f308f4e84fd0c915502ec32777d09b81e5382286977ae17" "basis" 1485 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-138901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "18{e}^{2t}-12t"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc8f891e15b0533a7f308f4e84fd0c915502ec32777d09b81e5382286977ae17" "basis" 1485 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-138901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "24{e}^{2t}-12t"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc8f891e15b0533a7f308f4e84fd0c915502ec32777d09b81e5382286977ae17" "basis" 1485 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-138901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "4{e}^{2t}-6t"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc8f891e15b0533a7f308f4e84fd0c915502ec32777d09b81e5382286977ae17" "basis" 1485 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-138901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12{e}^{2t}-18t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc8f891e15b0533a7f308f4e84fd0c915502ec32777d09b81e5382286977ae17" "basis" 1485 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-345339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $1\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the position of the particle is given by $s(t)=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ meters.  Find the expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc8f891e15b0533a7f308f4e84fd0c915502ec32777d09b81e5382286977ae17" "basis" 1485 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-345339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc8f891e15b0533a7f308f4e84fd0c915502ec32777d09b81e5382286977ae17" "basis" 1485 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-345339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7ad419d93ee9149ffc25caab33e9435e5bfce01f2e6915a31407868c03eb4de" "basis" 1485 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-345339" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc8f891e15b0533a7f308f4e84fd0c915502ec32777d09b81e5382286977ae17" "basis" 1485 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-345339" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\cos(t)-t\\sin(t)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc8f891e15b0533a7f308f4e84fd0c915502ec32777d09b81e5382286977ae17" "basis" 1485 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-138769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-36t+2$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc8f891e15b0533a7f308f4e84fd0c915502ec32777d09b81e5382286977ae17" "basis" 1485 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-138769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-36t+2) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-6t-36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2}-6t-36 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-t-6 & =0 \\\\ (t-3)(t+2) & =0 \\\\ t & =-2,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc8f891e15b0533a7f308f4e84fd0c915502ec32777d09b81e5382286977ae17" "basis" 1485 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-138769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7ad419d93ee9149ffc25caab33e9435e5bfce01f2e6915a31407868c03eb4de" "basis" 1485 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-138769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc8f891e15b0533a7f308f4e84fd0c915502ec32777d09b81e5382286977ae17" "basis" 1485 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-138770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $1.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)=2{t}^{3}-12{t}^{2}+t-5$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc8f891e15b0533a7f308f4e84fd0c915502ec32777d09b81e5382286977ae17" "basis" 1485 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-138770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-12{t}^{2}+t-5) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-24t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2}-24t+1) \\\\  & =12t-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12t-24 & =0 \\\\ 12 & =24 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc8f891e15b0533a7f308f4e84fd0c915502ec32777d09b81e5382286977ae17" "basis" 1485 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-138770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7ad419d93ee9149ffc25caab33e9435e5bfce01f2e6915a31407868c03eb4de" "basis" 1485 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-138770" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc8f891e15b0533a7f308f4e84fd0c915502ec32777d09b81e5382286977ae17" "basis" 1485 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-9454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $3 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N}).$\nAt time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t) =t^2- e^{t-4}$ meters per second.\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=4$ seconds." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/example-9454.json" "file_sha256" "f71da4f1c2fc407d1998b2dd890380e7406db7bb3322011ab4dfa5055c98f0bb" "basis" 1485 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-9454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-{e}^{t-4}) \\\\  & =2t-{e}^{t-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=4 \\, \\textrm{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =2(4)-{e}^{4-4} \\\\  & =8-1 \\\\  & =7\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =3\\cdot (7) \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F| = |21| = 21 \\, \\textrm{N}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/example-9454.json" "file_sha256" "f71da4f1c2fc407d1998b2dd890380e7406db7bb3322011ab4dfa5055c98f0bb" "basis" 1485 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-9859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $2 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t) = e^{3t-6} + t^2$ meters. Find the expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/example-9859.json" "file_sha256" "361f8c5571fb1280746723e6c5cb10cc5768005dbc1838a3cecbfae7c6606d06" "basis" 1485 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-9859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t-6}+{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{3t-6}+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{e}^{3t-6}+2t) \\\\  & =9{e}^{3t-6}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force acting on the body, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =2\\cdot (9{e}^{3t-6}+2) \\\\  & =18{e}^{3t-6}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/example-9859.json" "file_sha256" "361f8c5571fb1280746723e6c5cb10cc5768005dbc1838a3cecbfae7c6606d06" "basis" 1485 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-9860" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $2 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ moves in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\geq 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t) =2t^3-12t^2-30t+5$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time when the force acting on the body has a magnitude of zero." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/example-9860.json" "file_sha256" "3ce321173ce72369bddc1265594f4025062236cfd5e94dcd2f886a41e6884eb6" "basis" 1485 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-9860" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-12{t}^{2}-30t+5) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-24t-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2}-24t-30 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-4t-5 & =0 \\\\ (t+1)(t-5) & =0 \\\\ t & =-1,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t \\geq 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude when $t=5 \\, \\textrm{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/example-9860.json" "file_sha256" "3ce321173ce72369bddc1265594f4025062236cfd5e94dcd2f886a41e6884eb6" "basis" 1485 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-345340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the position of the particle is given by $s(t)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(t)$ meters.  Find the expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec01de86dc671147c3ff4c53a678e721e7396bac70cff16cd42c68ccb34bc824" "basis" 2334 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-345340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[{({t}^{2}+1)}^{-1}] \\\\  & =-{({t}^{2}+1)}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =-\\frac{2t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [-\\frac{2t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec01de86dc671147c3ff4c53a678e721e7396bac70cff16cd42c68ccb34bc824" "basis" 2334 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-345340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5cec7fba097a02c39cb6919b642068593a7870c37d6aba2f00c5f23ee199f33b" "basis" 2334 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-345340" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec01de86dc671147c3ff4c53a678e721e7396bac70cff16cd42c68ccb34bc824" "basis" 2334 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069047/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-345206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $1\\,\\text{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+3)$ meters per second.  Find an expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+3)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a90fd3d105e3cbb33b1883624925040a7d6746ee6dd42466371e16eef2adc8ca"} {"question" "q-345206" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345206" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2t}{t^2+3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345207" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $8\\,\\text{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=t\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)$ meters per second.  Find an expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=8\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)\\,+$\n\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345207" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)+t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)+t\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)+\\frac{8t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345207" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a90fd3d105e3cbb33b1883624925040a7d6746ee6dd42466371e16eef2adc8ca"} {"question" "q-345207" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345207" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{8t}{\\sqrt{1-t^2}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-138889" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $8\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}).$\nAt time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=4\\sqrt{t}-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-3)$ meters per second.\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=4$ seconds." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-138889" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\sqrt{t}-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-3)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}}-\\frac{5}{t-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=4\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4}}-\\frac{5}{4-3} \\\\  & =1-5 \\\\  & =-4\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F|=|-32|=32\\,\\text{N}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-138889" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a90fd3d105e3cbb33b1883624925040a7d6746ee6dd42466371e16eef2adc8ca"} {"question" "q-138889" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-138889" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "16\\,\\text{N}"] ["math" "24\\,\\text{N}"] ["math" "28\\,\\text{N}"] ["math" "32\\,\\text{N}"] ["math" "36\\,\\text{N}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-138889" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "32\\,\\text{N}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345227" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}).$  At time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=4{e}^{\\frac{t}{2}}$ meters per second. \n\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ seconds. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe magnitude of the force equals\n{{field-1}} $\\text{N}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345227" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{e}^{\\frac{t}{2}}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{\\frac{t}{2}}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\frac{t}{2}} \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\frac{t}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =2{e}^{\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}} \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =2(3) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =ma(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)|=|1|=1\\,\\text{N}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345227" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a90fd3d105e3cbb33b1883624925040a7d6746ee6dd42466371e16eef2adc8ca"} {"question" "q-345227" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345227" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-315648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=2{t}^{2}+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ meters per second.  Find an expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-315648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =4t+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (4t+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =16t+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-315648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a90fd3d105e3cbb33b1883624925040a7d6746ee6dd42466371e16eef2adc8ca"} {"question" "q-315648" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-315648" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "16t+12\\cos(t)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $1\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the position of the particle is given by $s(t)=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ meters.  Find the expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a90fd3d105e3cbb33b1883624925040a7d6746ee6dd42466371e16eef2adc8ca"} {"question" "q-345339" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345339" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\cos(t)-t\\sin(t)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the position of the particle is given by $s(t)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(t)$ meters.  Find the expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[{({t}^{2}+1)}^{-1}] \\\\  & =-{({t}^{2}+1)}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =-\\frac{2t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [-\\frac{2t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a90fd3d105e3cbb33b1883624925040a7d6746ee6dd42466371e16eef2adc8ca"} {"question" "q-345340" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345340" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{-t}{(1+t^2)^2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-138900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $1.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)={t}^{4}-{t}^{3}$ meters.\nFind the expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-138900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =12{t}^{2}-6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force in Newtons is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =1.5\\cdot (12{t}^{2}-6t) \\\\  & =18{t}^{2}-9t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-138900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a90fd3d105e3cbb33b1883624925040a7d6746ee6dd42466371e16eef2adc8ca"} {"question" "q-138900" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-138900" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12{t}^{2}+6t"] ["math" "16{t}^{2}+12t"] ["math" "18{t}^{2}-9t"] ["math" "24{t}^{2}-12t"] ["math" "9{t}^{2}-3t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-138900" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "18{t}^{2}-9t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-138901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)={e}^{2t}-{t}^{3}$ meters.\nFind the expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-138901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}-{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}-3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{2t}-3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{2t}-6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force in Newtons is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =3\\cdot (4{e}^{2t}-6t) \\\\  & =12{e}^{2t}-18t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-138901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a90fd3d105e3cbb33b1883624925040a7d6746ee6dd42466371e16eef2adc8ca"} {"question" "q-138901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-138901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12{e}^{2t}-18t"] ["math" "12{e}^{2t}-6t"] ["math" "18{e}^{2t}-12t"] ["math" "24{e}^{2t}-12t"] ["math" "4{e}^{2t}-6t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-138901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12{e}^{2t}-18t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-315735" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}).$  At time $t>0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)={t}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ meters.\n\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=2$ seconds. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe magnitude of the force equals\n{{field-1}} $\\text{N}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-315735" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\  & =2t+\\frac{4}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+\\frac{4}{t}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=2\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(2) & =2-\\frac{4}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force in Newtons is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2) & =ma(2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F(2)|=|2|=2\\,\\text{N}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-315735" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a90fd3d105e3cbb33b1883624925040a7d6746ee6dd42466371e16eef2adc8ca"} {"question" "q-315735" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-315735" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-138769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-36t+2$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-138769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-36t+2) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-6t-36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2}-6t-36 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-t-6 & =0 \\\\ (t-3)(t+2) & =0 \\\\ t & =-2,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-138769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a90fd3d105e3cbb33b1883624925040a7d6746ee6dd42466371e16eef2adc8ca"} {"question" "q-138769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-138769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t=1\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=2\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=3\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=5\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=6\\,\\text{s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-138769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t=3\\,\\text{s}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-138770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $1.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)=2{t}^{3}-12{t}^{2}+t-5$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-138770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-12{t}^{2}+t-5) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-24t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2}-24t+1) \\\\  & =12t-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12t-24 & =0 \\\\ 12 & =24 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-138770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a90fd3d105e3cbb33b1883624925040a7d6746ee6dd42466371e16eef2adc8ca"} {"question" "q-138770" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-138770" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t=2\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=3\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=4\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=5\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=7\\,\\text{s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-138770" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t=2\\,\\text{s}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345388" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=(2t-9){e}^{-2t}$ meters per second.\n\nCalculate the time ${t}_{∗}$ at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude. Do not round the answer.\n\n${t}_{∗}=$\n\n{{field-1}}  $\\text{s}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345388" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2t-9){e}^{-2t}] \\\\  & =(2t-9)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-2t})+{e}^{-2t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-9) \\\\  & =(2t-9)(-2{e}^{-2t})+2{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =(-4t+18){e}^{-2t}+2{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =(-4t+20){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4t+20){e}^{-2t} & =0 \\\\ -4t+20 & =0 \\\\ -4t & =-20 \\\\ t & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at ${t}_{∗}=5\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345388" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a90fd3d105e3cbb33b1883624925040a7d6746ee6dd42466371e16eef2adc8ca"} {"question" "q-345388" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345388" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345382" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $1\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}-8t$ meters per second.\n\nCalculate the time ${t}_{∗}$ at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude. Do not round the answer.\n\n${t}_{∗}=$\n\n{{field-1}}  $\\text{s}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345382" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}-8t) \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}-8 & =0 \\\\ 2{t}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =4 \\\\ t & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at ${t}_{∗}=2\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345382" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a90fd3d105e3cbb33b1883624925040a7d6746ee6dd42466371e16eef2adc8ca"} {"question" "q-345382" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345382" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345391" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons. At time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)=\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{4}+\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+6t+12$ meters.\n\nCalculate the time ${t}_{∗}$ at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude. Do not round the answer.\n\n${t}_{∗}=$\n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{s}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345391" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{4}+\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+6t+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-6t+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-6t+6) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+t-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+t-6 & =0 \\\\ (t-2)(t+3) & =0 \\\\ t & =-3,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at ${t}_{∗}=2\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345391" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a90fd3d105e3cbb33b1883624925040a7d6746ee6dd42466371e16eef2adc8ca"} {"question" "q-345391" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "q-345391" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d812edec3ae830574fb1670966a10e48ccf353c6f270540443c7350a4863ba"} {"question" "e-9454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $3 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N}).$\nAt time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t) =t^2- e^{t-4}$ meters per second.\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=4$ seconds." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/example-9454.json" "file_sha256" "f4b4e8d1e7f7d776021b19c80ca9e83e98e509f5db5a16dff5732c98e05c532a"} {"question" "e-9454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-{e}^{t-4}) \\\\  & =2t-{e}^{t-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=4 \\, \\textrm{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =2(4)-{e}^{4-4} \\\\  & =8-1 \\\\  & =7\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =3\\cdot (7) \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F| = |21| = 21 \\, \\textrm{N}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/example-9454.json" "file_sha256" "f4b4e8d1e7f7d776021b19c80ca9e83e98e509f5db5a16dff5732c98e05c532a"} {"question" "e-9859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $2 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t) = e^{3t-6} + t^2$ meters. Find the expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/example-9859.json" "file_sha256" "339381bcfdf948664bd2006935878bc29cda3ea7de739b6212f02e7ab1ac2d40"} {"question" "e-9859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t-6}+{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{3t-6}+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{e}^{3t-6}+2t) \\\\  & =9{e}^{3t-6}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force acting on the body, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =2\\cdot (9{e}^{3t-6}+2) \\\\  & =18{e}^{3t-6}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/example-9859.json" "file_sha256" "339381bcfdf948664bd2006935878bc29cda3ea7de739b6212f02e7ab1ac2d40"} {"question" "e-9860" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $2 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ moves in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\geq 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t) =2t^3-12t^2-30t+5$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time when the force acting on the body has a magnitude of zero." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/example-9860.json" "file_sha256" "afecab96f6b573dec05a888508965450b62455d3b434cb27247fc1ae3da55fc1"} {"question" "e-9860" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-12{t}^{2}-30t+5) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-24t-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2}-24t-30 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-4t-5 & =0 \\\\ (t+1)(t-5) & =0 \\\\ t & =-1,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t \\geq 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude when $t=5 \\, \\textrm{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071729/example-9860.json" "file_sha256" "afecab96f6b573dec05a888508965450b62455d3b434cb27247fc1ae3da55fc1"}] "usage" {"q-138901" [] "q-345340" [] "e-9454" [] "q-138769" [] "e-9859" [] "e-9860" [] "q-138770" [] "q-345339" []} "basis" 2574 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [17592186149059 17592186149060 17592186149061 17592186149062 17592186149063 17592186149065 17592186149066 17592186149067 17592186149068 17592186149069 17592186310664 17592186310665 17592186310666 17592186310667 17592186310668 17592186310716 17592186359117 17592186359118] "immutable_fields" [[17592186149058 :answer-field/correct] [17592186149058 :answer-field/id] [17592186149058 :answer-field/key] [17592186149058 :answer-field/type] [17592186149064 :answer-field/correct] [17592186149064 :answer-field/id] [17592186149064 :answer-field/key] [17592186149064 :answer-field/type] [17592186310663 :ans